All of them? Some, certainly, but all of them?

[unsnip list]
-All nine Nazgul rings collected
Stupid trick.

-Cannibal conduct (eat only flesh of own race)
Stupid trick, I guess. Effectively it's a slightly less more lenient
version of foodless conduct. But then again veggie and vegan conducts
are subsets of foodless too, and they're tracked by the game.

-Zen conducts? (I think these are pretty rare though)
Trick? Probably. Stupid? Depends on your definition. Most of us would
be stupid to try it, but anyone would be bloody clever if they
succeeded.

-Elberethless conduct
Is to illiterate what cannibal is to foodless, so call it a stupid
trick.

-number of artifacts collected/ generated/held at endgame?
Stupid trick.

-Beach bum conduct
Stupid trick.

-Tinned unique monsters/ unique statues in inventory
Stupid trick.

-Actual number of puddings killed (beyond the point where the game
loses count)
I thought it might be interesting or useful to see at a glance whether
a player simply extincted puddings or actively farmed them. I wasn't
suggesting a "who can kill most puddings" competition.

-Ludios created/ cleared?
Don't know if I'd call this a stupid trick, but given the low-risk
status of Ludios I guess it is kind of frivolous.

-Izchak survival
Actually, after posting I did start to think I had my own objections to
this. i wouldn't call leaving Izchak alive a stupid trick, but I think
respecting Izchak should be something suggested (sometimes forcefully)
by the community and then left up to the conscience of the player, not
enforced by the game.

-Ascended with original alignment or HoOA/conversion?
Again, I thought this might be an interesting piece of game information
that would help players to compare games. A player who ascended under a
HoOA is a player who didn't feel confident wading back into Astral to
find the correct altar. Or perhaps someone who hadn't ticked "lawful
rogue" or "chaotic valkyrie" off their ascension list. Or maybe just a
player who felt they got a rough deal from their own deity throughout
the game. Either way, it gives scoreboards a little more depth.



